  "notes": "An onomatopoeic する-verb derived from the チン sound (ding) that Japanese microwaves make when finished heating. Extremely common in casual speech — most Japanese people use チンする rather than the formal {電子|でんし}レンジで{温|あたた}める.\n\nCOMMON COLLOCATIONS:\n- チンして{食|た}べる: to microwave and eat\n- チンするだけ: just microwave it (describing easy meals)\n- レンジでチンする: to ding it in the microwave\n\nUSAGE:\nUsed in casual, everyday conversation. Not appropriate in formal writing. The word is understood by virtually all Japanese speakers regardless of age. Convenience store staff may ask: {温|あたた}めますか？ (Shall I heat it up?) — the formal equivalent of チンする.\n\nRELATED WORDS:\n- {電子|でんし}レンジ: microwave oven (the formal word for the appliance)\n- {温|あたた}める: to heat up, to warm (the formal verb)",
